        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Sanxingdui culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>. Key sites: Sanxingdui, about 4 km north-east of Nanxing Township in Guanghan (Deyang), on the Yazi River, and the related <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinsha-site">Jinsha site</a> 40 km away.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Periods II–III of the Sanxingdui site, contemporary with the Shang dynasty; the walled city was founded c. 1600 BCE and the objects in the pits are radiocarbon-dated to the 12th–11th centuries BCE. Period I belongs to the Baodun culture.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">bronze masks and heads with protruding eyes; the world’s oldest life-sized standing human statue; a bronze tree 3.96 m high with birds; gold masks; jade cong and zhang; sacrificial pits; a walled city of 3.6 square kilometres ringed by canals</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Chance finds from 1927 and excavations from 1934, above all the pits found in 1986 and 2020–2022; radiocarbon dating; much later Chinese texts on the kingdom of Shu (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Shujing</a>, the Chronicles of Huayang). No written records of its own survive.</span></div>
        </div>
