    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Sanxingdui</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sichuan.jpg" alt="Sichuan" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Sichuan <span class="kid-tale-cr">chensiyuan, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 3,600 years ago, people built a walled city at Sanxingdui, in Sichuan in south-west China.</p><p>Wide canals ran around its thick walls, for watering fields, for boats and for defence.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bronze-casting.jpg" alt="Bronze casting" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Bronze casting <span class="kid-tale-cr">José-Manuel Benito, CC BY-SA 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>Its workers cast big bronze masks and heads with bulging eyes and large ears.</p><p>They also made the world's oldest life-sized standing statue of a person, and a bronze tree almost 4 metres high.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cancong.jpg" alt="Cancong" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cancong <span class="kid-tale-cr">Tyg728, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Much later, books told of a kingdom called Shu, whose first ruler, Cancong, had eyes that stuck out.</p><p>Many Chinese archaeologists think Sanxingdui belonged to this early kingdom of Shu.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silk.jpg" alt="Silk" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Silk <span class="kid-tale-cr">Kuebi = Armin Kübelbeck, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 3,000 years ago, gold, bronze and jade objects were broken, burned and carefully buried in pits.</p><p>New pits dug between 2020 and 2022 held a gold mask, ivory and traces of silk.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jinsha-site.jpg" alt="Jinsha site" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Jinsha site <span class="kid-tale-cr">xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>No one knows how the culture ended, but the Shu capital may have moved to Jinsha, 40 km away.</p><p>Today its treasures are shown in the Sanxingdui Museum, near where they were found.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Sanxingdui was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> culture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, contemporary with the Shang dynasty, that cast bronzes in a style of its own: great masks with bulging eyes, a life-sized standing figure and a bronze tree almost 4 m high.</li><li>Its people built a walled city of 3.6 square kilometres, ringed by canals and divided into districts, and the culture is thought to have been governed by a strong central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocracy">theocracy</a>.</li><li>Many Chinese archaeologists identify it with the early kingdom of Shu, whose legendary founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cancong">Cancong</a> was said to have protruding eyes like those of the bronze masks.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Paint survives on the bronze faces, black on the large eyes and eyebrows and vermilion on the lips, nostrils and ear holes, which the sinologist Corinne Debaine-Francfort reads as offerings for the head to taste, smell and hear.</p></aside>
    </section>

